WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.116 --> 00:00:02.868
Hello and welcome back to J D Science.

2
00:00:03.042 --> 00:00:05.944
Today, we are exploring how bacteria grow.

3
00:00:06.153 --> 00:00:09.857
Bacteria reproduce by binary fission, an asexual

4
00:00:09.915 --> 00:00:10.472
process.

5
00:00:10.681 --> 00:00:14.408
If they doubled every twenty minutes, one bacterium would become two

6
00:00:14.524 --> 00:00:18.309
after twenty minutes, four after forty, eight after sixty, and

7
00:00:18.379 --> 00:00:18.843
so on.

8
00:00:19.052 --> 00:00:22.338
Growth is fastest with the right temperature and nutrients.

9
00:00:22.547 --> 00:00:25.890
In the lab, we grow bacteria on agar in a petri dish.

10
00:00:26.134 --> 00:00:29.872
We sterilise the loop and dish to stop unwanted microbes, and we

11
00:00:29.930 --> 00:00:33.854
tape the lid loosely and store the dish upside down so condensation

12
00:00:33.924 --> 00:00:35.944
does not drip onto the agar.

13
00:00:36.769 --> 00:00:40.542
In school labs we do not culture pathogens at thirty-seven degrees

14
00:00:40.588 --> 00:00:44.454
Celsius, because that is the temperature that suits human pathogens

15
00:00:44.512 --> 00:00:44.988
best.

16
00:00:45.441 --> 00:00:48.587
To calculate growth, use the number of generations.

17
00:00:48.982 --> 00:00:52.709
Divide the total time by the doubling time, then multiply the

18
00:00:52.767 --> 00:00:56.378
starting number of bacteria by two to the power of that number.

19
00:00:56.656 --> 00:01:00.372
For example, over two hours with a twenty-minute doubling time,

20
00:01:00.499 --> 00:01:02.032
there are six generations.

21
00:01:02.183 --> 00:01:05.793
One bacterium could therefore become sixty-four, provided

22
00:01:05.851 --> 00:01:07.442
conditions remain suitable.

23
00:01:07.779 --> 00:01:12.005
School microbiology work must follow the laboratory's risk assessment

24
00:01:12.063 --> 00:01:14.095
and approved incubation procedures.

25
00:01:14.617 --> 00:01:17.938
As you revise this topic, try to explain the key process in

26
00:01:18.008 --> 00:01:18.936
your own words.

27
00:01:19.215 --> 00:01:22.942
Focus on what happens, why it happens, and how the structures

28
00:01:23.023 --> 00:01:24.834
involved help the process work.

29
00:01:25.287 --> 00:01:28.549
Now, pause the video and try this exam-style question.

30
00:01:29.002 --> 00:01:32.369
Explain why the lid of a petri dish should be taped loosely

31
00:01:32.613 --> 00:01:34.586
and the dish stored upside down.

32
00:01:35.294 --> 00:01:38.290
[two marks] Here is a model answer.

33
00:01:38.499 --> 00:01:42.284
To stop unwanted microbes getting in while allowing air in.

34
00:01:42.620 --> 00:01:45.871
Upside down prevents condensation dripping onto the agar.

35
00:01:46.208 --> 00:01:49.842
If this explanation has helped, please subscribe to J D Science

36
00:01:49.877 --> 00:01:50.898
for more lessons.

37
00:01:51.142 --> 00:01:55.275
You can find further learning resources and practice questions at JDscience

38
00:01:55.368 --> 00:01:56.703
dot co dot U K.

39
00:01:56.796 --> 00:01:57.841
Thank you for watching.
